WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
A cell sits in a secreted signal and thrives, yet does it truly need that signal?

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.860
To find out, block the signal outside the cell.

3
00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:17.003
One tool is a neutralizing antibody, a protein built to bind one target.

4
00:00:18.303 --> 00:00:22.203
Added outside, it catches the free signal before it reaches the cell.

5
00:00:23.503 --> 00:00:26.563
The receptors stay empty, so the message inside fades.

6
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:33.213
A second tool is a decoy receptor, a free receptor fragment with no cell attached.

7
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:36.253
Why does a receptor block signaling?

8
00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:42.193
Its binding pocket soaks up the free signal, leaving the cell's real receptors

9
00:00:42.193 --> 00:00:43.193
empty.

10
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:46.043
You can also block the receptor itself.

11
00:00:47.423 --> 00:00:51.583
An antibody caps the pocket, or a short peptide plugs it from outside.

12
00:00:53.023 --> 00:00:57.803
Every blocker works the same way: keep signal and receptor apart, and watch what

13
00:00:57.803 --> 00:00:58.803
fails.

14
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:04.947
Test this with nerve growth factor, or NGF, which acts on sympathetic ganglia,

15
00:01:05.387 --> 00:01:06.547
clusters of nerve cells.

16
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:12.487
A sample holds NGF and a second factor that opens eyelids early.

17
00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:19.127
You inject newborn mice with the sample plus antiserum, serum carrying antibodies

18
00:01:19.127 --> 00:01:24.067
against pure NGF. What happens to their ganglia and eyelids?

19
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.580
The antiserum traps the sample's NGF and the mouse's own, so most sympathetic

20
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.780
ganglia are destroyed.

21
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.460
Cohen raised it; Levi-Montalcini and Booker saw this in newborn mice.

22
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.380
Yet the eyelids still open early, because the second factor slips past untouched.

23
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:52.543
A blocker can mislead if you miss dose or timing.

24
00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:57.663
The same antiserum has much less effect in older animals.

25
00:01:59.083 --> 00:02:02.543
Sensory neurons that need NGF are hit hardest earlier.

26
00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:09.963
In 1980, Johnson and colleagues destroyed rat sensory neurons with maternal antibody

27
00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:14.943
before birth. Test at the wrong age, and a real need looks absent.

28
00:02:14.943 --> 00:02:15.943
Test at the wrong age, and a real need.

29
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:18.547
Overlap can also hide a signal's role.

30
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:23.727
When two receptors share a job, blocking one lets some signal through.

31
00:02:24.987 --> 00:02:29.487
So experimenters pair blocking with adding the factor, and check against genetics.

32
00:02:29.487 --> 00:02:37.227
In 2001, Fukuchi-Shimogori and Grove paired added FGF8 with a receptor-fragment

33
00:02:37.227 --> 00:02:38.227
blocker.

34
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:42.147
With those checks, outside blockers unlocked discoveries.

35
00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:49.847
In 1984, Pierschbacher and Ruoslahti found RGD, fibronectin's cell-binding handle.

36
00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:55.767
Free RGD peptide fills the cell's grip site, so fewer cells hold on.

37
00:02:57.027 --> 00:03:03.547
In 1988, Caroni and Schwab's antibody IN-1 neutralized myelin proteins that block

38
00:03:03.547 --> 00:03:08.687
nerve growth. Its larger antigen was later cloned as Nogo-A.

39
00:03:09.717 --> 00:03:16.297
Ogawa, Mikoshiba and colleagues added antibody CR-50 to embryonic cortical cell

40
00:03:16.297 --> 00:03:22.257
cultures. Normal tissue then organized like tissue from reeler, a mutant mouse.

41
00:03:23.617 --> 00:03:29.917
In 1997, two groups together showed CR-50 recognizes Reelin, a secreted

42
00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:35.357
glycoprotein. Catch the signal or plug its receptor, and you test the need.
